WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.310
&gt;&gt; In the last few
years, actually,

00:00:08.311 --> 00:00:11.820
I've been slowly
getting obsessed with

00:00:11.821 --> 00:00:16.365
this particular topic
of Smart Cities.

00:00:16.366 --> 00:00:19.330
It was just accidental
that I started into this,

00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:23.610
but I think it's turning out
to be a very interesting,

00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:25.080
I think rich space for

00:00:25.081 --> 00:00:26.790
a lot of interesting
research problems,

00:00:26.791 --> 00:00:28.815
which is what we are
interested in, but also,

00:00:28.816 --> 00:00:31.245
an opportunity to really shape

00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:34.940
how these things will get
implemented in India right now.

00:00:34.941 --> 00:00:42.100
So, I'll just try to present

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.050
coherent story on
what we could do as

00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:53.575
Technologists for enabling

00:00:53.576 --> 00:00:58.945
really futuristic Smart Cities
solutions in India.

00:00:58.946 --> 00:01:02.580
So, really one of
the main things which we

00:01:02.581 --> 00:01:04.320
have been trying to make a case

00:01:04.321 --> 00:01:06.655
for is like an open stack,

00:01:06.656 --> 00:01:10.190
which will really
allow for a rich set

00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:13.950
of applications to develop
and emerge in the future.

00:01:13.951 --> 00:01:18.020
So, to motivate this,

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:20.210
what I'll do is I'll go a little

00:01:20.211 --> 00:01:23.750
bit in detail into
this recent RFP,

00:01:23.751 --> 00:01:26.190
which has been put out
by the City of Agra,

00:01:26.191 --> 00:01:29.820
which I'm sure Betel Nolkia
would be able to tell you

00:01:29.821 --> 00:01:31.680
in much greater detail of

00:01:31.681 --> 00:01:34.630
many of the other
aspects of this RFP.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.710
We'll just focus on

00:01:37.711 --> 00:01:41.970
the technology related aspects
of this particular RFP.

00:01:41.971 --> 00:01:44.865
I presume that this is

00:01:44.866 --> 00:01:47.965
a prototypical RFP
for a Smart City,

00:01:47.966 --> 00:01:51.370
which is similar to a
lot of what other cities

00:01:51.371 --> 00:01:55.125
are doing or want to be done.

00:01:55.126 --> 00:01:58.240
If you see out here,
one of the key elements

00:01:58.241 --> 00:02:01.015
out here is this integrated
control and command center.

00:02:01.016 --> 00:02:03.880
So, clearly they want
a visibility as to

00:02:03.881 --> 00:02:07.460
the overall operations
of the city.

00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:09.760
There are a number of
components which can plug

00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:12.620
into this command
and Control Center.

00:02:12.621 --> 00:02:14.866
Transportation,

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.980
intelligent traffic
management system,

00:02:17.981 --> 00:02:19.770
solid waste management,

00:02:19.771 --> 00:02:26.250
surveillance, interactions
with the citizens of the city,

00:02:26.251 --> 00:02:27.480
being able to communicate through

00:02:27.481 --> 00:02:29.855
digital signages, and so on.

00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:33.585
So, these are all the
various components,

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:36.180
which they would like to have

00:02:36.181 --> 00:02:40.155
executed in the RFP,
which they have given up.

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:43.500
So, what we thought was
let us go into this RFP

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:45.375
a little bit more detail
to understand

00:02:45.376 --> 00:02:47.760
from a technology perspective.

00:02:47.761 --> 00:02:49.970
What are the key elements

00:02:49.971 --> 00:02:52.680
which will need to be supported?

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.780
So, the RFP also says
that you need to have

00:02:57.781 --> 00:03:00.735
a layered approach and
this is common sense.

00:03:00.736 --> 00:03:02.820
In some sense, especially with

00:03:02.821 --> 00:03:06.465
all the sensors being
deployed like cameras etc.,

00:03:06.466 --> 00:03:08.520
they advocate having
these multiple layers

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.745
out here, sensor layer,

00:03:09.746 --> 00:03:13.770
communication layer, data
layer and application layer.

00:03:13.771 --> 00:03:16.070
What I would like to focus on,

00:03:16.071 --> 00:03:18.025
as we go forward in this talk

00:03:18.026 --> 00:03:21.080
is essentially this layer,
the data layer,

00:03:21.081 --> 00:03:22.965
which I believe is like

00:03:22.966 --> 00:03:25.305
the core fundamental layer which

00:03:25.306 --> 00:03:27.930
we really need to make sure

00:03:27.931 --> 00:03:31.455
it's designed and
constructed well

00:03:31.456 --> 00:03:33.105
because it will really enable

00:03:33.106 --> 00:03:35.525
all kinds of interesting
things to happen in the top,

00:03:35.526 --> 00:03:37.660
once if that is done well.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.320
So, if you look at

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:46.820
this adaptive
traffic management system,

00:03:46.821 --> 00:03:49.950
what they would really
like to do is to be able

00:03:49.951 --> 00:03:55.710
to manage the traffic in a
reactive, in a smart way.

00:03:55.711 --> 00:03:57.870
For example, they
want to be able to

00:03:57.871 --> 00:04:00.000
control the traffic
signals optimally,

00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:03.905
so that it can change as
required based on the duration,

00:04:03.906 --> 00:04:06.465
on the time of the day, and
the traffic conditions.

00:04:06.466 --> 00:04:08.360
I guess this is

00:04:08.361 --> 00:04:10.480
a big deal in India, we
are happy the way it goes.

00:04:10.481 --> 00:04:12.460
So, they want to be able to

00:04:12.461 --> 00:04:15.940
automatically create these
corridors for VIP vehicles,

00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:18.400
as well as emergency,
and police vehicles.

00:04:18.401 --> 00:04:20.260
They would like to actually have

00:04:20.261 --> 00:04:23.120
incident messaging being
done in real time.

00:04:23.121 --> 00:04:24.940
Also, there are little

00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:26.200
forward-looking in the
sense that they're

00:04:26.201 --> 00:04:27.880
saying let us collect this data

00:04:27.881 --> 00:04:29.140
of all the traffic conditions,

00:04:29.141 --> 00:04:30.880
make it available, so that

00:04:30.881 --> 00:04:32.990
it can be used for
further analysis,

00:04:32.991 --> 00:04:35.865
and improvement of
the road networks.

00:04:35.866 --> 00:04:39.520
So, if we look at the
implication on the data layer,

00:04:39.521 --> 00:04:42.375
this real timeness is

00:04:42.376 --> 00:04:44.140
important which emerges
when you want to

00:04:44.141 --> 00:04:46.455
quickly be able to connect
to various things.

00:04:46.456 --> 00:04:48.910
Also, you want to be
able to, of course,

00:04:48.911 --> 00:04:50.230
it's not immediately
apparent from here,

00:04:50.231 --> 00:04:52.850
but if you look at
the RFP detail,

00:04:52.851 --> 00:04:55.210
it's also apparent that
you want to be able to

00:04:55.211 --> 00:04:57.450
ingest data from
a diversity of sources,

00:04:57.451 --> 00:05:01.885
cameras, crowdsource, police
database systems, etc.

00:05:01.886 --> 00:05:03.940
Similarly, you want to
make the data available to

00:05:03.941 --> 00:05:06.205
a diversity of applications,

00:05:06.206 --> 00:05:07.975
and it's not just this adaptive

00:05:07.976 --> 00:05:09.600
traffic management
system application,

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:12.080
but maybe other applications.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.370
They have this,

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:31.140
yeah?

00:05:31.141 --> 00:05:34.000
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:05:34.001 --> 00:05:35.395
&gt;&gt; That is a very good point

00:05:35.396 --> 00:05:37.330
and I don't have
an answer to that.

00:05:37.331 --> 00:05:40.060
I mean, if you don't have
enough capacity of the road,

00:05:40.061 --> 00:05:43.160
I mean there's not much you
can do in terms of that.

00:05:43.161 --> 00:05:44.770
But, hopefully, you can at

00:05:44.771 --> 00:05:46.565
least do some demand
response type thing.

00:05:46.566 --> 00:05:48.050
You can stagger how

00:05:48.051 --> 00:05:51.355
people- titles cannot even
know the capacity by,

00:05:51.356 --> 00:05:54.155
it's creating incentive
schemes, and so on.

00:05:54.156 --> 00:05:58.840
&gt;&gt; You don't study [inaudible].

00:05:58.841 --> 00:06:00.420
Because you are not. You
are already spending

00:06:00.421 --> 00:06:04.330
money on something
that is zero result.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:09.010
&gt;&gt; So, that is one view
is that why do all

00:06:09.011 --> 00:06:11.990
these when we can't even
make a proper footpath.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.850
Were not even able to walk
properly and we want to fly.

00:06:15.851 --> 00:06:19.240
But, maybe we cannot

00:06:19.241 --> 00:06:22.480
bypass this like what
we did with wireless,

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:24.560
it directly gone to smartphone by

00:06:24.561 --> 00:06:27.295
passing the intermediate layers.

00:06:27.296 --> 00:06:29.430
So, the other thing is that,

00:06:29.431 --> 00:06:31.880
I think one of

00:06:31.881 --> 00:06:35.215
the things also which is
embedded in the RFP turns out,

00:06:35.216 --> 00:06:39.605
is that just making the
information available

00:06:39.606 --> 00:06:41.810
hopefully will allow citizens

00:06:41.811 --> 00:06:43.870
to say look what the heck
are you doing.

00:06:43.871 --> 00:06:46.330
Are you doing your job?

00:06:46.331 --> 00:06:47.820
Why aren't you fix
this and so on.

00:06:47.821 --> 00:06:50.105
So, that also hopefully
there'll be some positive

00:06:50.106 --> 00:06:52.670
comes sort of just making
the data available.

00:06:52.671 --> 00:06:57.150
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:06:57.151 --> 00:07:00.390
&gt;&gt; Right. Yes. Agra, I

00:07:00.391 --> 00:07:02.770
don't think- as it's
said everything-

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.335
&gt;&gt; Bike.

00:07:04.336 --> 00:07:06.695
&gt;&gt; Bicycles, okay. So, you

00:07:06.696 --> 00:07:08.290
have to also get
the appropriating challenge.

00:07:08.291 --> 00:07:14.290
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:07:14.291 --> 00:07:16.560
&gt;&gt; There are a lot of
interesting, okay.

00:07:16.561 --> 00:07:19.600
So, I'll point out
something funny out here.

00:07:19.601 --> 00:07:21.430
So, intelligent
traffic NCC turbulent.

00:07:21.431 --> 00:07:23.400
So, traffic comes again
in two different places,

00:07:23.401 --> 00:07:25.340
adapter traffic as well
as as part of this,

00:07:25.341 --> 00:07:28.585
but I'm just reporting
what they set out there.

00:07:28.586 --> 00:07:30.175
Here, they really want to

00:07:30.176 --> 00:07:31.580
have the system to
support the police.

00:07:31.581 --> 00:07:32.470
So, this from the point of view

00:07:32.471 --> 00:07:33.760
of the police's perspective,

00:07:33.761 --> 00:07:35.600
they want to be able
to traffic police,

00:07:35.601 --> 00:07:38.430
be able to give finds,

00:07:38.431 --> 00:07:41.935
do traffic management,
monitor suspicious people,

00:07:41.936 --> 00:07:44.505
vital places, video analytics.

00:07:44.506 --> 00:07:46.220
They want to be able to detect

00:07:46.221 --> 00:07:49.370
no helmet whether someone
smoking in the car,

00:07:49.371 --> 00:07:52.570
this is funny wrongly
driving detection.

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:54.700
So, they want that.
I don't know why.

00:07:54.701 --> 00:07:56.450
They would like to actually also

00:07:56.451 --> 00:07:59.460
detective people have
long hair or short hair.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:03.240
It's there, this RFP.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:10.180
So, this is interesting.

00:08:10.181 --> 00:08:13.630
They would like to have a video
coming from ad-hoc video,

00:08:13.631 --> 00:08:15.610
so similar you just set
up a video network,

00:08:15.611 --> 00:08:16.950
and then data should
come from there,

00:08:16.951 --> 00:08:19.170
or variable like police is
wearing a video camera,

00:08:19.171 --> 00:08:21.480
data should be coming
from there, and so on.

00:08:21.481 --> 00:08:22.830
Then, of course,
they want this to

00:08:22.831 --> 00:08:24.480
empower secure communications.

00:08:24.481 --> 00:08:26.865
So, what this implies
is for the data layer,

00:08:26.866 --> 00:08:29.150
you need to support
end-to-end security,

00:08:29.151 --> 00:08:32.140
and also diversity
of radio sources,

00:08:32.141 --> 00:08:34.765
mobile, wearable,
ad-hoc type sources.

00:08:34.766 --> 00:08:38.254
You need to really support
the diversity of analytics,

00:08:38.255 --> 00:08:40.130
which might not be
doable right now,

00:08:40.131 --> 00:08:42.810
and see their point is some
of these longer, shorter,

00:08:42.811 --> 00:08:44.725
it might take time,
they want that,

00:08:44.726 --> 00:08:46.660
but in the future,

00:08:46.661 --> 00:08:48.380
you'll have more
requirements for analytics,

00:08:48.381 --> 00:08:49.285
and then of course

00:08:49.286 --> 00:08:52.210
strong authorization
and authentication.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.635
Solid waste management, they

00:08:55.636 --> 00:08:59.560
have this probably for
most of the cities.

00:08:59.561 --> 00:09:02.010
They don't know what is going on,

00:09:02.011 --> 00:09:04.060
where people actually
collecting the waste.

00:09:04.061 --> 00:09:06.350
It's supposed to either
disposing of properly and so on.

00:09:06.351 --> 00:09:08.000
So, you just need
more visibility by putting

00:09:08.001 --> 00:09:09.770
sensors to track vehicles,

00:09:09.771 --> 00:09:11.860
monitor waste collection sites,

00:09:11.861 --> 00:09:15.100
manage the roots of
this trucks, etc.,

00:09:15.101 --> 00:09:16.760
look at the handover from

00:09:16.761 --> 00:09:18.235
one small truck to
the bigger truck,

00:09:18.236 --> 00:09:19.790
what is going on,
things like that,

00:09:19.791 --> 00:09:21.830
and really increase automation.

00:09:21.831 --> 00:09:25.610
Again, out here diversity data

00:09:25.611 --> 00:09:27.515
ingestion from
a diversity of sources

00:09:27.516 --> 00:09:29.460
followed by video analytics.

00:09:29.461 --> 00:09:30.530
They also want video analytics.

00:09:30.531 --> 00:09:31.700
They want to see
what is happening in

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:34.370
the monitoring waste
disposal sites,

00:09:34.371 --> 00:09:36.865
are things being done
properly or not.

00:09:36.866 --> 00:09:39.290
They would like all of
this to be reported,

00:09:39.291 --> 00:09:40.370
summary to be reported to

00:09:40.371 --> 00:09:42.730
the integrated command
and Control Center.

00:09:42.731 --> 00:09:46.110
GIS is a big deal for this,

00:09:46.111 --> 00:09:50.585
I guess most of
the smart city RFPs,

00:09:50.586 --> 00:09:52.170
where they would like to use this

00:09:52.171 --> 00:09:54.360
for better revenue
management, and planning,

00:09:54.361 --> 00:09:56.475
and urban development, and

00:09:56.476 --> 00:09:58.280
maintenance of
public places, and so on.

00:09:58.281 --> 00:10:00.000
They would like this
to get integrated with

00:10:00.001 --> 00:10:02.340
other smart solutions
like in surveillance.

00:10:02.341 --> 00:10:06.030
So, GIS is a important
requirement for the data layer,

00:10:06.031 --> 00:10:08.610
being able to provide GIS data,

00:10:08.611 --> 00:10:10.530
and space data, I'll
come to this what I

00:10:10.531 --> 00:10:12.990
mean by this little
later in the talk.

00:10:12.991 --> 00:10:15.660
So, that you can do a correlation
analysis of data from

00:10:15.661 --> 00:10:20.860
all these different sensors
in the context of GIS data.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.380
Air quality, noise, light,

00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:28.215
and being able to report that

00:10:28.216 --> 00:10:30.550
through messaging displays is
something they would like,

00:10:30.551 --> 00:10:34.120
and they would also want
these to be tamper-proof.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:38.480
So, this is where it's

00:10:38.481 --> 00:10:39.770
interesting some of
the requirements are

00:10:39.771 --> 00:10:41.210
coming up in
the specific components,

00:10:41.211 --> 00:10:42.580
but they are genderly
or they cannot cut

00:10:42.581 --> 00:10:46.690
across the entire
implementation of smart cities.

00:10:46.691 --> 00:10:49.400
So, secure onboarding of
devices is important,

00:10:49.401 --> 00:10:50.945
so that you can trust

00:10:50.946 --> 00:10:52.290
the device which is
sending the data,

00:10:52.291 --> 00:10:54.770
and detect if the
trust is broken.

00:10:54.771 --> 00:10:57.605
You would like to know how

00:10:57.606 --> 00:10:59.840
healthy these devices
are and then really,

00:10:59.841 --> 00:11:01.775
again, coming back to
this exchanging data

00:11:01.776 --> 00:11:04.830
between sensors and
other applications.

00:11:04.831 --> 00:11:10.862
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]?

00:11:10.863 --> 00:11:14.250
&gt;&gt; I guess, they want
to measure the amount

00:11:14.251 --> 00:11:18.995
of noise decibel levels
in these places,

00:11:18.996 --> 00:11:21.910
just monitor that, I guess.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:25.350
I guess, if you had these
microphones you could also do

00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:29.890
some analytics with that in
conjunction with the video,

00:11:29.891 --> 00:11:31.040
though they're not asking for it,

00:11:31.041 --> 00:11:32.100
but you could do

00:11:32.101 --> 00:11:33.660
some interesting things
with that I guess.

00:11:33.661 --> 00:11:42.905
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]?

00:11:42.906 --> 00:11:46.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I don't know. I'm sure

00:11:46.071 --> 00:11:48.605
there are certain positive
laws, I don't know.

00:11:48.606 --> 00:11:50.110
I don't know actually. See, it's

00:11:50.111 --> 00:11:51.170
like the air quality, right?

00:11:51.171 --> 00:11:52.810
I mean, there are
certain safe air

00:11:52.811 --> 00:11:55.200
quality levels to be maintained,

00:11:55.201 --> 00:11:58.310
but we're kind of not
really maintaining that,

00:11:58.311 --> 00:12:00.590
so probably, the same
happens with these lines.

00:12:00.591 --> 00:12:02.810
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:12:02.811 --> 00:12:04.355
&gt;&gt; Taj? Okay. I see.

00:12:04.356 --> 00:12:07.550
Yeah. So, Taj Mahal,

00:12:07.551 --> 00:12:10.320
so that is driving some
of these requirements.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:15.890
I guess, once you start having

00:12:15.891 --> 00:12:17.060
data, see that is the thing,

00:12:17.061 --> 00:12:18.520
is that maybe you can kind of use

00:12:18.521 --> 00:12:20.810
that to drive some actions,

00:12:20.811 --> 00:12:24.040
policy actions after
that, around that.

00:12:24.041 --> 00:12:25.950
So, for example, this kind

00:12:25.951 --> 00:12:27.670
of data would also be
useful to correlate

00:12:27.671 --> 00:12:29.540
with the health implications

00:12:29.541 --> 00:12:31.355
of the people living
in those areas.

00:12:31.356 --> 00:12:32.690
So, we don't have data but

00:12:32.691 --> 00:12:34.110
once we start having
this kind of data,

00:12:34.111 --> 00:12:36.580
those kind of studies can
also be done and then we

00:12:36.581 --> 00:12:39.050
could inform the city,

00:12:39.051 --> 00:12:43.280
could do the appropriate things
in terms of, yes.

00:12:43.281 --> 00:12:47.870
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:12:47.871 --> 00:12:49.560
&gt;&gt; For noise?

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:51.485
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:12:51.486 --> 00:12:53.270
&gt;&gt; Noise, I don't
know, but I do know

00:12:53.271 --> 00:12:55.360
that maybe the same people,

00:12:55.361 --> 00:12:57.750
because air quality
related studies,

