00:27:27.936 --> 00:27:30.790
I feel that it should be local at

00:27:30.791 --> 00:27:32.260
a level of a ward of a locality

00:27:32.261 --> 00:27:33.985
at a campus or some such thing.

00:27:33.986 --> 00:27:35.840
Then that's what I'll kind of

00:27:35.841 --> 00:27:37.905
discuss in the next few slides.

00:27:37.906 --> 00:27:40.240
So that you don't

00:27:40.241 --> 00:27:42.600
really ship this huge amounts
of data all over the place,

00:27:42.601 --> 00:27:44.010
it just consume there,

00:27:44.011 --> 00:27:45.080
and then that's why you need the

00:27:45.081 --> 00:27:46.970
Edge Analytics also
running there.

00:27:46.971 --> 00:27:50.020
It's only the alert
that are transient.

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:54.510
So, this data exchange
layer needs to have,

00:27:54.511 --> 00:27:55.965
what are the attributes for this.

00:27:55.966 --> 00:27:57.510
We need to have
very strong security,

00:27:57.511 --> 00:27:59.880
handle heterogeneous data
across video,

00:27:59.881 --> 00:28:00.920
and other IoT data.

00:28:00.921 --> 00:28:03.935
These are the key functionality
of supporting exchange

00:28:03.936 --> 00:28:08.055
of data between various
applications and devices.

00:28:08.056 --> 00:28:10.460
Be a repository of data models,

00:28:10.461 --> 00:28:12.560
because just raw data by
itself is not useful.

00:28:12.561 --> 00:28:14.495
What is the metadata
about the data,

00:28:14.496 --> 00:28:16.340
and that is something

00:28:16.341 --> 00:28:18.180
else we insisted on
that every source of

00:28:18.181 --> 00:28:20.020
data comes with
metadata information so

00:28:20.021 --> 00:28:22.205
that you know what it means,

00:28:22.206 --> 00:28:25.855
support low-latency,
have APIs, and SDKs.

00:28:25.856 --> 00:28:30.630
Because of the need for
a local just for video,

00:28:30.631 --> 00:28:33.460
if it is not video, probably
Edge Analytics might not be

00:28:33.461 --> 00:28:36.820
that useful but maybe other
than for latency reasons.

00:28:36.821 --> 00:28:38.660
But definitely, video requires

00:28:38.661 --> 00:28:41.685
I think Fog/Edge Analytics.

00:28:41.686 --> 00:28:43.170
Now, what was missing here,

00:28:43.171 --> 00:28:45.400
we would like to also add
these three requirements that,

00:28:45.401 --> 00:28:47.230
they should be strong
protections for privacy.

00:28:47.231 --> 00:28:48.800
Say if someone is,

00:28:48.801 --> 00:28:51.160
you should know as a citizen with

00:28:51.161 --> 00:28:53.895
all cameras or whatever
video feeds you appear in,

00:28:53.896 --> 00:28:55.800
I mean that would
be nice to know.

00:28:55.801 --> 00:28:57.740
Then you need to have

00:28:57.741 --> 00:29:00.050
a marketplace to be able
to buy and sell data,

00:29:00.051 --> 00:29:03.245
and of course support the
AI type applications.

00:29:03.246 --> 00:29:06.290
I'll kind of briefly mention
what I mean by that.

00:29:06.291 --> 00:29:11.195
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] There
is no privacy anymore.

00:29:11.196 --> 00:29:13.750
You are telling me I have
the right information.

00:29:13.751 --> 00:29:16.080
&gt;&gt; Correct. Yeah. At
least be able to know.

00:29:16.081 --> 00:29:20.590
You see because if we
take that forward.

00:29:20.591 --> 00:29:24.200
Suppose cameras support but,

00:29:24.201 --> 00:29:27.875
maybe you lack the data

00:29:27.876 --> 00:29:30.745
in which you appear
or what you own.

00:29:30.746 --> 00:29:33.070
That is unlocked
only based on, yeah,

00:29:33.071 --> 00:29:47.640
&gt;&gt; Sorry. [inaudible]
. Is data privacy.

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:52.095
Supposed to deliver
it in index form,

00:29:52.096 --> 00:29:54.000
which is what's
taking into account

00:29:54.001 --> 00:29:56.180
the fact that GDPR in one end

00:29:56.181 --> 00:30:00.345
is extremely strict and in
the US we have control.

00:30:00.346 --> 00:30:02.760
Are they supposed to
be in [inaudible]

00:30:02.761 --> 00:30:05.250
and that protection
on top of that,

00:30:05.251 --> 00:30:07.410
there is going to be a law.

00:30:07.411 --> 00:30:12.825
That's the end of my question.

00:30:12.826 --> 00:30:14.730
&gt;&gt; Just additional information.

00:30:14.731 --> 00:30:16.620
These cases where in Seattle

00:30:16.621 --> 00:30:19.440
a system was abused by asking for

00:30:19.441 --> 00:30:20.700
so much information by

00:30:20.701 --> 00:30:25.620
the city authority to
satisfy those requests.

00:30:25.621 --> 00:30:27.930
Then there's the issue
I think money products

00:30:27.931 --> 00:30:32.130
where if you have access
to excessive data,

00:30:32.131 --> 00:30:35.190
you are building a cost on
someone to deliver it to you.

00:30:35.191 --> 00:30:38.805
So, is there any thinking
of how we will manage

00:30:38.806 --> 00:30:45.555
excess and sustainability
of cost for?

00:30:45.556 --> 00:30:48.080
&gt;&gt; I think those are
all opportunities

00:30:48.081 --> 00:30:49.110
for startups to offer

00:30:49.111 --> 00:30:52.540
services along those lengths.
That's what I think.

00:30:52.541 --> 00:30:54.790
So for example,

00:30:54.791 --> 00:30:57.810
Analytics-as-a-Service,
Annotation-as-a-Service,

00:30:57.811 --> 00:31:00.510
all these are
interesting opportunities

00:31:00.511 --> 00:31:04.780
to think of coming up
with solutions for those.

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:07.665
That's where these kinds of

00:31:07.666 --> 00:31:09.230
data stacks which
are very open will

00:31:09.231 --> 00:31:13.220
support those kinds of new kinds
of services to come up.

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:17.125
So, this is the stack,

00:31:17.126 --> 00:31:18.770
basically what we're saying,

00:31:18.771 --> 00:31:21.520
and it's not very

00:31:21.521 --> 00:31:22.750
different from what any of

00:31:22.751 --> 00:31:24.735
these IoT type stacks look like,

00:31:24.736 --> 00:31:27.625
except there are
a few things out here.

00:31:27.626 --> 00:31:29.280
Of course, you have
the brokers for

00:31:29.281 --> 00:31:31.320
streaming messages
and alerts and so on,

00:31:31.321 --> 00:31:33.505
which is an archive,

00:31:33.506 --> 00:31:35.570
being able to access
these archived versions

00:31:35.571 --> 00:31:36.650
of these messages.

00:31:36.651 --> 00:31:38.760
Also, support for
real-time messaging,

00:31:38.761 --> 00:31:40.530
but the video and audio,

00:31:40.531 --> 00:31:42.820
the media brokers
and databases are

00:31:42.821 --> 00:31:46.235
also part of the stack
so that you access this.

00:31:46.236 --> 00:31:50.065
Basically, a directory of

00:31:50.066 --> 00:31:52.060
data resources out here which is

00:31:52.061 --> 00:31:54.370
a catalog which has
all the meta information.

00:31:54.371 --> 00:31:56.685
Can I give you an example of
how that might look like,

00:31:56.686 --> 00:31:59.120
support for certain
contexts because if you

00:31:59.121 --> 00:32:01.410
want to really interpret
the data coming in,

00:32:01.411 --> 00:32:02.670
you need to know
what is the context,

00:32:02.671 --> 00:32:04.380
so that you can do
your inferences and so on.

00:32:04.381 --> 00:32:07.455
So, that is what I
meant by AI support.

00:32:07.456 --> 00:32:10.845
A specific example, I'll
just talk about the spaces.

00:32:10.846 --> 00:32:12.840
Then, which is also very

00:32:12.841 --> 00:32:15.200
important in the context
of video and Edge

00:32:15.201 --> 00:32:16.490
Analytics is that you
need to be able to

00:32:16.491 --> 00:32:18.350
support running of analytics and

00:32:18.351 --> 00:32:20.420
not this support to run

00:32:20.421 --> 00:32:24.200
analytics at the Fog
and the Edge layer.

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:29.020
Then as a framework
for authentication,

00:32:29.021 --> 00:32:33.335
authorization, provisioning
et cetera, as part of this.

00:32:33.336 --> 00:32:36.200
So this is basically the stack

00:32:36.201 --> 00:32:40.085
and we want to- and all
this is exposed as APIs,

00:32:40.086 --> 00:32:42.650
and each of them
runs as services or

00:32:42.651 --> 00:32:45.310
microservices in some sense

00:32:45.311 --> 00:32:47.790
from an implementation
perspective.

00:32:47.791 --> 00:32:51.045
So if you look at from
a deployment option

00:32:51.046 --> 00:32:52.965
you have the stack at the center,

00:32:52.966 --> 00:32:55.780
and currently what will

00:32:55.781 --> 00:32:57.050
probably happen is
that you already

00:32:57.051 --> 00:32:58.400
might have applications running.

00:32:58.401 --> 00:32:59.750
Waste Management, this
is an application

00:32:59.751 --> 00:33:01.190
someone has developed et cetera.

00:33:01.191 --> 00:33:04.370
So, with Adapters you put
data into the stack and

00:33:04.371 --> 00:33:06.760
the command and Control
Center will pull data off

00:33:06.761 --> 00:33:08.130
the stack in some sense

00:33:08.131 --> 00:33:09.665
and also communicate
through this.

00:33:09.666 --> 00:33:12.920
So, this is how probably
the immediate future

00:33:12.921 --> 00:33:18.185
with legacy or ongoing
applications things will work out.

00:33:18.186 --> 00:33:21.940
But maybe going forward
you cannot decouple

00:33:21.941 --> 00:33:24.050
the data producing portion

00:33:24.051 --> 00:33:25.470
from the data
consumption portion.

00:33:25.471 --> 00:33:27.940
Even the data producing
person is just

00:33:27.941 --> 00:33:31.120
done by a bunch of companies,

00:33:31.121 --> 00:33:34.915
consuming is done by some
other kind bunch of companies,

00:33:34.916 --> 00:33:37.190
and all of them work through

00:33:37.191 --> 00:33:39.605
the Data Exchange Stack
in some sense.

00:33:39.606 --> 00:33:45.410
So, what you can see from

00:33:45.411 --> 00:33:46.710
the Command and Control Center is

00:33:46.711 --> 00:33:47.920
not just the data but also

00:33:47.921 --> 00:33:49.600
the health of the devices and

00:33:49.601 --> 00:33:51.595
health of all the things
producing the data.

00:33:51.596 --> 00:33:54.490
That is essentially
the management systems VMS,

00:33:54.491 --> 00:33:56.910
Element Management System,
Network Management System.

00:33:56.911 --> 00:33:58.710
They also report the health of

00:33:58.711 --> 00:34:00.800
all these different things

00:34:00.801 --> 00:34:02.900
to the Command and
Control Center.

00:34:02.901 --> 00:34:07.030
Okay. When we think

00:34:07.031 --> 00:34:09.270
in terms of that is kind
of a software perspective,

00:34:09.271 --> 00:34:10.820
when you look at
the hardware perspective

00:34:10.821 --> 00:34:13.050
what you will have is

00:34:13.051 --> 00:34:15.010
a collection of gateways or

00:34:15.011 --> 00:34:17.510
IoT gateways which are out
there publicly deployed,

00:34:17.511 --> 00:34:19.500
but possibly on street poles,

00:34:19.501 --> 00:34:21.280
because those are
something which are

00:34:21.281 --> 00:34:23.780
all over the city they
have access to power.

00:34:23.781 --> 00:34:26.420
So you have these
street poles where

00:34:26.421 --> 00:34:29.415
each street pole has
this IoT gateway with a bunch of

00:34:29.416 --> 00:34:33.620
locally wired connected sensors

00:34:33.621 --> 00:34:35.440
and actuators and
perhaps also ingesting

00:34:35.441 --> 00:34:37.735
data from nearby
sensors wirelessly.

00:34:37.736 --> 00:34:40.640
All these street poles
are connected through

00:34:40.641 --> 00:34:43.935
a WAN to the local
Micro Data Center.

00:34:43.936 --> 00:34:45.920
So this could be at
the level of a campus

00:34:45.921 --> 00:34:48.370
or a ward or locality.

00:34:48.371 --> 00:34:53.250
This Micro Data Center actually
has the WAN terminator,

00:34:53.251 --> 00:34:54.860
network switches and then compute

00:34:54.861 --> 00:34:56.875
and storage services for it.

00:34:56.876 --> 00:34:59.860
So this becomes the what
is called the Fog load,

00:34:59.861 --> 00:35:01.180
some people call it the fog,

00:35:01.181 --> 00:35:03.290
they have different
terminologies.

00:35:03.291 --> 00:35:05.160
But this is the Micro
Data Center sitting

00:35:05.161 --> 00:35:09.995
there serving a
locality out here.

00:35:09.996 --> 00:35:17.320
So we expect this

00:35:17.321 --> 00:35:19.139
to be the hardware realization

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:23.930
of the smart city solutions.

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:26.430
Anyway if you do it that way you

00:35:26.431 --> 00:35:30.045
can do it in a modular fashion,

00:35:30.046 --> 00:35:33.730
each locality gets lit up
in some sense or IoTified.

00:35:33.731 --> 00:35:36.420
Then you have all these
Micro Data Centers

00:35:36.421 --> 00:35:37.760
which are then managed

00:35:37.761 --> 00:35:39.920
or visible as in a unified way

00:35:39.921 --> 00:35:42.120
through Integrated Command
and Control Centers.

00:35:42.121 --> 00:35:45.620
So this way you slowly
lighter different portions

00:35:45.621 --> 00:35:48.885
of the city on
a step-by-step basis.

00:35:48.886 --> 00:35:54.040
The software itself you

00:35:54.041 --> 00:35:55.390
would want to of course

00:35:55.391 --> 00:35:57.500
make it run in
a distributed fashion.

00:35:57.501 --> 00:36:01.790
So, what here I'm showing
is that the core,

00:36:01.791 --> 00:36:03.570
the API gateways and

00:36:03.571 --> 00:36:05.130
the authentication et cetra is in

00:36:05.131 --> 00:36:06.840
some sense unified
and centralized.

00:36:06.841 --> 00:36:10.900
But the stack itself the brokers

00:36:10.901 --> 00:36:13.095
et cetera are run in
a distributed fashion

00:36:13.096 --> 00:36:16.255
each in the local
Micro Data Center.

00:36:16.256 --> 00:36:18.510
So that way the whole thing

00:36:18.511 --> 00:36:21.500
is actually distributed, okay.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:25.625
So, there are
a few concepts which

00:36:25.626 --> 00:36:27.920
we want to bring out here which

00:36:27.921 --> 00:36:30.280
are I think interesting

00:36:30.281 --> 00:36:32.775
and maybe some of them
are new in some sense.

00:36:32.776 --> 00:36:34.870
So one is of course
a Registry of Data Sources.

00:36:34.871 --> 00:36:38.120
Now this is something
which OCF is also there

00:36:38.121 --> 00:36:40.160
multiple approaches
to our efforts to

00:36:40.161 --> 00:36:42.630
try to come up with a schema,

00:36:42.631 --> 00:36:45.595
a standard framework for
capturing the metadata.

00:36:45.596 --> 00:36:48.785
So we will look at what OCF did,

00:36:48.786 --> 00:36:53.100
and I were proposing
something along those lines.

00:36:53.101 --> 00:36:55.360
Where any IoT source or

00:36:55.361 --> 00:36:57.095
any data source has a schema

00:36:57.096 --> 00:36:59.240
which has four pieces
of information.

00:36:59.241 --> 00:37:00.510
There's some static information,

00:37:00.511 --> 00:37:02.110
there is some schema

00:37:02.111 --> 00:37:04.320
about the observation data
which comes in schema with

00:37:04.321 --> 00:37:06.470
the control data which
goes to the device and

00:37:06.471 --> 00:37:10.270
then schema for
the configuration data.

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:14.580
As an example out here
one of the elements

00:37:14.581 --> 00:37:17.180
is the output of lux sensor

00:37:17.181 --> 00:37:19.110
which is of type number which is

00:37:19.111 --> 00:37:22.815
measuring the LED output
intensity, units of lux.

00:37:22.816 --> 00:37:26.690
It has what we're also
seeing is that there is

00:37:26.691 --> 00:37:28.095
an access modifier to

00:37:28.096 --> 00:37:30.265
control the privacy
setting out here.

00:37:30.266 --> 00:37:32.570
So you have private data,

00:37:32.571 --> 00:37:34.655
you have protected data,
you have public data.

00:37:34.656 --> 00:37:38.295
Private data is only accessible
to the owner of the data,

00:37:38.296 --> 00:37:40.390
whereas protected is data

00:37:40.391 --> 00:37:42.580
which is available to
someone who is authorized,

00:37:42.581 --> 00:37:45.870
and public is open to
available to everyone.

00:37:45.871 --> 00:37:48.270
Now, this kind of schema
that the idea is that

00:37:48.271 --> 00:37:50.930
any data source when
it gets registered

00:37:50.931 --> 00:37:53.120
in the system also has
to put in a schema

00:37:53.121 --> 00:37:57.220
which tells you about the format
of the data coming in.

00:37:57.221 --> 00:37:59.510
Then the system can
validate the pieces

00:37:59.511 --> 00:38:01.850
of actual data which come
in against the schema

00:38:01.851 --> 00:38:06.930
that is what is shown out
here on the right side.

00:38:06.931 --> 00:38:09.715
So, as an example if
you take a street light

00:38:09.716 --> 00:38:12.995
which through of schema

00:38:12.996 --> 00:38:15.300
which is a proposal
schema which we

00:38:15.301 --> 00:38:21.780
have experimenting with in
our reference implementation,

00:38:21.781 --> 00:38:23.760
which gives you
some meta information,

00:38:23.761 --> 00:38:24.910
this is a static portion.

00:38:24.911 --> 00:38:27.295
Where is the street light
located? Who owns it?

00:38:27.296 --> 00:38:29.670
How do you access the data
from the street light which

00:38:29.671 --> 00:38:31.930
is what does the
subscription endpoint?

00:38:31.931 --> 00:38:35.445
What is the API endpoint
for getting archival data?

00:38:35.446 --> 00:38:37.350
Then the data from
the street light

00:38:37.351 --> 00:38:39.310
itself what are
the different pieces of data

00:38:39.311 --> 00:38:41.120
which come in essentially when is

00:38:41.121 --> 00:38:43.180
like this a sampling instead.

00:38:43.181 --> 00:38:44.260
Which is the temperature of

00:38:44.261 --> 00:38:46.000
the casing,
the power consumption,

00:38:46.001 --> 00:38:47.130
the ambient lux et cetera,

00:38:47.131 --> 00:38:50.075
and then some meta information
describing each of those.

00:38:50.076 --> 00:38:52.080
So, this is the
control inflammation

00:38:52.081 --> 00:38:53.630
that is how do you go and change

00:38:53.631 --> 00:38:57.880
the brightness level for
example of the street light.

00:38:57.881 --> 00:39:00.820
So, the actual data from
the street light will of course

00:39:00.821 --> 00:39:04.000
be decent type documents,

00:39:04.001 --> 00:39:05.700
keyword values which
are adhering to

00:39:05.701 --> 00:39:08.950
that particular schema out there.

00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:12.700
So, that is the
first thing is that

00:39:12.701 --> 00:39:14.330
every piece of data has to have

00:39:14.331 --> 00:39:16.765
this data model
which is uploaded.

00:39:16.766 --> 00:39:18.700
Second is video itself, right?

00:39:18.701 --> 00:39:20.734
So now with video,

00:39:20.735 --> 00:39:24.620
we would like to also treat
video as an IoT element.

00:39:24.621 --> 00:39:28.775
What does it really mean for
video to be an IoT element?

00:39:28.776 --> 00:39:32.080
Well, we need to have some
metadata about the video,

00:39:32.081 --> 00:39:34.430
and metadata on the semantic
content of the video

00:39:34.431 --> 00:39:37.220
not just the frame rate
and resolution and so on.

00:39:37.221 --> 00:39:38.780
But what is inside these?

00:39:38.781 --> 00:39:39.960
Some description that we had

00:39:39.961 --> 00:39:42.640
this lux sensor and
the units are lux.

00:39:42.641 --> 00:39:45.575
Is there some other similar
information we can put

00:39:45.576 --> 00:39:47.090
maybe over the semantic content

00:39:47.091 --> 00:39:48.880
or some associated inflammation.

00:39:48.881 --> 00:39:50.605
I'll give you a simple example.

00:39:50.606 --> 00:39:55.820
So, for example if I look
out here someone go do

00:39:55.821 --> 00:39:58.100
an analytics on
this video and give out

00:39:58.101 --> 00:39:59.635
this meta data stream

00:39:59.636 --> 00:40:01.595
which is associated
with the video stream.

00:40:01.596 --> 00:40:04.195
Which tells me are there

00:40:04.196 --> 00:40:06.700
any Autos in this video sequence?

00:40:06.701 --> 00:40:08.965
How many diesel vehicles
are there? Et cetra.

00:40:08.966 --> 00:40:10.870
The thing is these kinds
of analytics then

00:40:10.871 --> 00:40:13.600
help the consumer to

00:40:13.601 --> 00:40:15.700
work off the analytics
data and not worry

00:40:15.701 --> 00:40:18.110
about the raw data anymore
or the encoded video stream.

00:40:18.111 --> 00:40:19.705
Now you're working on a derived

00:40:19.706 --> 00:40:21.710
information as you go forward.

00:40:21.711 --> 00:40:24.330
This will really enable
complex applications to be

00:40:24.331 --> 00:40:26.610
built where you build on
this kind of analytics.

00:40:26.611 --> 00:40:30.620
It'll also allow analytics
service providers who develop

00:40:30.621 --> 00:40:32.070
very specific analytics and offer

00:40:32.071 --> 00:40:36.175
them as analytic stream.

00:40:36.176 --> 00:40:39.530
So, the formatting is a
little messed up here.

00:40:39.531 --> 00:40:42.020
So for example, a
simple use cases,

00:40:42.021 --> 00:40:44.670
let us say you have a mobile
video source like a drone,

00:40:44.671 --> 00:40:46.440
and the drone is giving out

00:40:46.441 --> 00:40:48.690
the encoded video
stream but you'd

00:40:48.691 --> 00:40:52.250
like to know what are
the Geo-location,

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:54.940
as a time series,

00:40:54.941 --> 00:40:57.640
so that that is extra meta
information because you

00:40:57.641 --> 00:41:00.475
had that maybe Geo-location
maybe the posts of the drone,

00:41:00.476 --> 00:41:02.450
you can do better much more

00:41:02.451 --> 00:41:05.115
interesting inferences
with the video stream.

00:41:05.116 --> 00:41:07.420
So, it is just a simple schema
to describe that

00:41:07.421 --> 00:41:09.860
very similar to the schema
of the street light.

00:41:09.861 --> 00:41:11.760
Here I'm just looking
at the data portion

00:41:11.761 --> 00:41:13.600
of the schema which is telling me

00:41:13.601 --> 00:41:18.020
that this is the timestamp
of the metadata.

00:41:18.021 --> 00:41:20.740
You need to be able to refer
into the video stream,

00:41:20.741 --> 00:41:22.930
what portion of the video stream
is this piece of

00:41:22.931 --> 00:41:26.010
Meta Data actually matching to?

00:41:26.011 --> 00:41:27.940
Start an end pointer
of the video?

00:41:27.941 --> 00:41:30.100
Then what is actual data coming?

00:41:30.101 --> 00:41:31.390
Is just the latitude longitude

00:41:31.391 --> 00:41:32.720
in this particular example saying

00:41:32.721 --> 00:41:35.470
that this portion of

00:41:35.471 --> 00:41:38.375
the video frame this
is the Geo-location.

00:41:38.376 --> 00:41:40.425
So you have now a stream of,

00:41:40.426 --> 00:41:42.620
let's have this picture
again it tells you you have

00:41:42.621 --> 00:41:45.345
the video stream which is
going flowing out here.

00:41:45.346 --> 00:41:48.710
You have this Meta Data
corresponding to

00:41:48.711 --> 00:41:52.030
the video stream which is
flowing on the side of tier.

00:41:52.031 --> 00:41:53.475
So for example this piece of

00:41:53.476 --> 00:41:55.690
this Meta-Data Stream
each metadata packet

00:41:55.691 --> 00:41:57.850
actually is referring to
some portion of the video and

00:41:57.851 --> 00:42:01.130
then contain some information
about that video stream.

00:42:01.750 --> 00:42:04.130
So, this is one thing. So, have

00:42:04.131 --> 00:42:07.110
this Meta-Data stream which
is also in the catalog.

00:42:07.111 --> 00:42:09.510
So, any consumer will
know okay what is

00:42:09.511 --> 00:42:12.890
this derived
information available

00:42:12.891 --> 00:42:15.395
so you can build some new
applications on top of it.

00:42:15.396 --> 00:42:17.300
Secondly if you want
to subscribe to

00:42:17.301 --> 00:42:20.490
the video stream itself
you need to have a way

00:42:20.491 --> 00:42:22.255
of just like you
would subscribe to

00:42:22.256 --> 00:42:23.940
IoT Data Stream you

00:42:23.941 --> 00:42:25.795
need to be able to
subscribe to Video Streams.

00:42:25.796 --> 00:42:30.445
So you need to have
steaming servers,

00:42:30.446 --> 00:42:33.310
be able to access
archive streams et cetera,

00:42:33.311 --> 00:42:37.030
and the corresponding metadata
which comes in out here.

00:42:37.031 --> 00:42:38.920
So, one of the things

00:42:38.921 --> 00:42:41.110
is a lot of the
video especially for

00:42:41.111 --> 00:42:42.630
the surveillance
camera is awfully

00:42:42.631 --> 00:42:44.830
locals push
spatio-temporal relevance.

00:42:44.831 --> 00:42:47.035
No one else outset
really cares about it.

00:42:47.036 --> 00:42:53.905
So, I'm just going to
highlight here problem,

00:42:53.906 --> 00:42:55.800
we are thinking about
is how do you create

00:42:55.801 --> 00:42:58.395
this localized efficient
video distribution system

00:42:58.396 --> 00:43:00.000
for the local region.

00:43:00.001 --> 00:43:06.020
So, this I think is where
you have load balancing,

00:43:06.021 --> 00:43:08.235
you have caching of
these brokers etc done.

00:43:08.236 --> 00:43:10.960
For example, you're stuck
in a traffic junction,

00:43:10.961 --> 00:43:12.900
or you're stuck in the
road and you want to know

00:43:12.901 --> 00:43:14.640
what is happening ahead.
You're just curious.

00:43:14.641 --> 00:43:16.490
With your city app, you're
able to subscribe to

00:43:16.491 --> 00:43:19.365
a video camera which is
just ahead and you'll see,

00:43:19.366 --> 00:43:21.040
okay this is why there
is a problem there.

00:43:21.041 --> 00:43:22.705
You're just curious to know.

00:43:22.706 --> 00:43:24.350
This kind of a local consumption

00:43:24.351 --> 00:43:25.890
of video from a local source

00:43:25.891 --> 00:43:29.715
to be able to support
these things.

00:43:29.716 --> 00:43:32.290
Also, be able to kind of adapt

00:43:32.291 --> 00:43:34.610
the QOS of the local network.

00:43:34.611 --> 00:43:36.010
For example, if I have

00:43:36.011 --> 00:43:38.980
some incidents happening
in a local region,

00:43:38.981 --> 00:43:40.675
you probably won't allocate

00:43:40.676 --> 00:43:43.545
more bandwidth to the cameras
around that region.

00:43:43.546 --> 00:43:45.000
Maybe you want to
track a crowd as

00:43:45.001 --> 00:43:47.670
it's going past, right?

00:43:47.671 --> 00:43:50.160
So, adaptive QOS
for those things.

00:43:50.161 --> 00:43:52.310
So these are some of the,

00:43:52.311 --> 00:43:56.775
I would say interesting
RND problems.

00:43:56.776 --> 00:43:58.460
I'm not saying this
will be part of

00:43:58.461 --> 00:43:59.980
the stack which we're
doing but these are

00:43:59.981 --> 00:44:02.255
some interesting problems which

00:44:02.256 --> 00:44:05.650
are worth looking at
as we go forward.

00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:09.500
The thing with the
video is that you want

00:44:09.501 --> 00:44:11.345
to get and consume the video,

00:44:11.346 --> 00:44:13.415
and analyze the video locally.

00:44:13.416 --> 00:44:15.260
Right now, of
course, you do it at

00:44:15.261 --> 00:44:18.710
the Fog layer because those
a Standard Compute Servers,

00:44:18.711 --> 00:44:20.610
but I guess with
technologies which you guys

00:44:20.611 --> 00:44:22.590
are developing MSR you can also

00:44:22.591 --> 00:44:27.090
push that to the Edges out
here on the IoT gateways.

00:44:27.091 --> 00:44:29.070
Now the thing is
that this collective

00:44:29.071 --> 00:44:30.940
of the Micro Data Center,

00:44:30.941 --> 00:44:34.115
and this IoT gateway in
some sense forms cloud.

00:44:34.116 --> 00:44:36.555
I call it the street cloud.

00:44:36.556 --> 00:44:38.465
So, you have to come-
and street cloud

00:44:38.466 --> 00:44:40.370
has this heterogeneous
compute two sources.

00:44:40.371 --> 00:44:42.280
Little computes in
the IoT gateways and

00:44:42.281 --> 00:44:44.880
more compute in the
compute servers.

00:44:44.881 --> 00:44:49.970
So, basically you want

00:44:49.971 --> 00:44:51.080
to locally terminate the video

00:44:51.081 --> 00:44:52.690
both for privacy and bandwidth,

00:44:52.691 --> 00:44:54.915
and you want to
run this analytics

00:44:54.916 --> 00:44:57.555
out here in the street cloud.

00:44:57.556 --> 00:44:59.895
So, how do you do that?

00:44:59.896 --> 00:45:04.685
What is the programming model

00:45:04.686 --> 00:45:08.945
to allow you to develop
applications like this,

00:45:08.946 --> 00:45:11.210
in a general way and what kind

00:45:11.211 --> 00:45:13.960
of runtime is required
to support that?

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:17.030
So, this is something
again I think is

00:45:17.031 --> 00:45:19.820
an interesting systems problem

00:45:19.821 --> 00:45:23.310
to look at as you go forward.

00:45:25.010 --> 00:45:29.095
As an example, we have
been looking at how you

00:45:29.096 --> 00:45:30.530
could take advantage and do

00:45:30.531 --> 00:45:32.595
this Distributed Video Analytics.

00:45:32.596 --> 00:45:34.815
So, where you have

00:45:34.816 --> 00:45:36.310
a camera and you have

00:45:36.311 --> 00:45:38.300
some compute to the poll
right next to the camera,

00:45:38.301 --> 00:45:39.660
and then you have
more heavier duty

00:45:39.661 --> 00:45:41.795
compute out in
the Micro Data Center.

00:45:41.796 --> 00:45:45.795
So here what we have
explored is how you can do

00:45:45.796 --> 00:45:48.950
this tracking locally and

00:45:48.951 --> 00:45:51.910
the object detection out
in the Micro Data Center.

00:45:51.911 --> 00:45:55.030
So, this is some work
we did last year,

00:45:55.031 --> 00:46:00.155
where you have multiple cameras

00:46:00.156 --> 00:46:01.240
and then you have

00:46:01.241 --> 00:46:04.400
an object detector running
the Micro Data Center,

00:46:04.401 --> 00:46:06.900
and the D-frames are
the frames on which you

00:46:06.901 --> 00:46:09.210
do the object detection
in the data center,

00:46:09.211 --> 00:46:10.680
whereas the D-frames are done

00:46:10.681 --> 00:46:14.340
locally next to the camera
to do the tracking.

00:46:14.341 --> 00:46:18.210
So, the detector
of course runs is

00:46:18.211 --> 00:46:20.210
a much heavier duty detector
running on one of

00:46:20.211 --> 00:46:22.175
these neural nets and

00:46:22.176 --> 00:46:24.385
locally you do
the tracking et cetera.

00:46:24.386 --> 00:46:27.155
So, there is some benefit
to doing this,

00:46:27.156 --> 00:46:29.440
we reported last year.

00:46:29.441 --> 00:46:32.810
But, I think more
interesting question is how

00:46:32.811 --> 00:46:34.350
do you when you
write this because

00:46:34.351 --> 00:46:36.030
it's always a moving target,

00:46:36.031 --> 00:46:38.050
the amount of compute
you have every year is

00:46:38.051 --> 00:46:39.490
improving even in the Edge Node

00:46:39.491 --> 00:46:40.835
you can do more and more compute.

00:46:40.836 --> 00:46:44.210
So, you can't hard partition
your thing saying that,

00:46:44.211 --> 00:46:45.880
"What you couldn't do today maybe

00:46:45.881 --> 00:46:46.950
two years from now you can do

00:46:46.951 --> 00:46:48.390
easily in the Edge compute."

00:46:48.391 --> 00:46:49.980
So, how do we write

00:46:49.981 --> 00:46:52.380
these applications and break
it so that these pieces are

00:46:52.381 --> 00:46:55.615
able to move around easily

00:46:55.616 --> 00:46:58.090
and collectively they
do the job you want to?

00:46:58.091 --> 00:47:01.425
So that I think is also
very interesting problem.

00:47:01.426 --> 00:47:02.930
So, there is this,

00:47:02.931 --> 00:47:04.860
the third concept which
we kind of want to

00:47:04.861 --> 00:47:08.580
articulate out here is
concept of space as an asset.

00:47:08.581 --> 00:47:12.595
Space regions, and this is
something which actually

00:47:12.596 --> 00:47:16.710
Intel has brought
to our attention.

00:47:16.711 --> 00:47:19.205
OTR who are also part
of our collaborators.

00:47:19.206 --> 00:47:23.180
So, this particular
conference room

00:47:23.181 --> 00:47:25.780
is the space region which will
be there in the database.

00:47:25.781 --> 00:47:27.230
Because if you have sensors out

00:47:27.231 --> 00:47:28.680
there for example
the smoke detectors,

00:47:28.681 --> 00:47:29.940
you should be able to pin it to

00:47:29.941 --> 00:47:31.260
this particular space region

00:47:31.261 --> 00:47:34.365
which means this space region
needs to have a entity,

00:47:34.366 --> 00:47:37.010
a presence in your database

00:47:37.011 --> 00:47:39.060
with a specific ID so
that you can tie to that.

00:47:39.061 --> 00:47:40.980
It can't just be the Geolocation,

00:47:40.981 --> 00:47:44.455
because the Geolocation really
and not work well inside.

00:47:44.456 --> 00:47:47.220
So that is a concept where

00:47:47.221 --> 00:47:50.780
you have spaces
articulated as assets.

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:54.025
The thing is that for example,

00:47:54.026 --> 00:47:55.595
this space if you take,

00:47:55.596 --> 00:47:57.500
this is the map
just outside around

00:47:57.501 --> 00:48:00.005
this vicinity where you have
your Microsoft building,

00:48:00.006 --> 00:48:02.570
you have ITC gardenia, et cetera.

00:48:02.571 --> 00:48:04.465
So I've just marked out
three space region.

00:48:04.466 --> 00:48:07.055
So there is this SR1 which
is this piece of road,

00:48:07.056 --> 00:48:08.970
SR2 is your Microsoft building,

00:48:08.971 --> 00:48:11.310
SR3 is this another
building out here.

00:48:11.311 --> 00:48:15.405
These regions have to
be captured somehow.

00:48:15.406 --> 00:48:17.870
Of course, in the database
you will have

00:48:17.871 --> 00:48:21.050
SR1 as a region which
has a unique ID,

00:48:21.051 --> 00:48:22.925
it's described by
the street segment

00:48:22.926 --> 00:48:24.430
owned by the city of Bangalore,

00:48:24.431 --> 00:48:25.930
administered by BBMP et cetera.

00:48:25.931 --> 00:48:28.080
Similarly, you have associated

00:48:28.081 --> 00:48:30.035
information for every regions,

00:48:30.036 --> 00:48:31.445
but more importantly you have

00:48:31.446 --> 00:48:33.875
a structure which connects
these space regions together.

00:48:33.876 --> 00:48:39.045
Saying that, SR1 is adjacent
to SR2, SR3, et cetera,

00:48:39.046 --> 00:48:42.635
and SR1 is part of this bigger
Space Region called road,

00:48:42.636 --> 00:48:44.985
RajaRamMohanRoad and so on.

00:48:44.986 --> 00:48:46.660
Now, why is this interesting?

00:48:46.661 --> 00:48:48.925
Because you can use

00:48:48.926 --> 00:48:51.540
this structure to build
your application.

00:48:51.541 --> 00:48:52.830
For example, suppose
there is an incident

00:48:52.831 --> 00:48:54.140
which happens in this SR1

00:48:54.141 --> 00:48:57.300
which is the road segment
just in front of you.

00:48:57.301 --> 00:49:00.780
Who are the people who need
to be informed about that?

00:49:00.781 --> 00:49:04.020
So, what I want to just
build a general application

00:49:04.021 --> 00:49:06.875
without customizingly doing
it for this particular.

00:49:06.876 --> 00:49:09.180
So, where then what you would do

00:49:09.181 --> 00:49:11.715
is you take that you
look at the network

00:49:11.716 --> 00:49:13.210
in this graph structure

00:49:13.211 --> 00:49:14.990
and traverse that
and figure out what

00:49:14.991 --> 00:49:16.980
all the affected people who

00:49:16.981 --> 00:49:19.720
get notified about that accident.

00:49:19.721 --> 00:49:25.610
So, this is the context

00:49:25.611 --> 00:49:28.060
what I meant by saying
supporting AI services,

00:49:28.061 --> 00:49:30.320
is that you capture the context.

00:49:30.321 --> 00:49:34.360
This context is most likely
in the form of a graph.

00:49:34.361 --> 00:49:36.340
It's not just be contexts of

00:49:36.341 --> 00:49:37.740
the space region it could also be

00:49:37.741 --> 00:49:39.250
context which is related

00:49:39.251 --> 00:49:41.280
to this organizational
structures.

00:49:41.281 --> 00:49:44.310
So the're many things out
here which are useful,

00:49:44.311 --> 00:49:46.675
it's probably should be captured.

00:49:46.676 --> 00:49:50.620
So, I think that the challenge
here is to populate

00:49:50.621 --> 00:49:53.370
the database with such kind
of information in

00:49:53.371 --> 00:49:56.135
a maybe automated way perhaps
a semi-automated way.

00:49:56.136 --> 00:50:00.195
So that you have a rich database
which you can now

00:50:00.196 --> 00:50:01.610
start using to do more and more

00:50:01.611 --> 00:50:05.080
interesting automated
DI type applications.

00:50:05.081 --> 00:50:06.890
So, but at a minimum at

00:50:06.891 --> 00:50:08.530
least what we are
saying is let us

00:50:08.531 --> 00:50:10.180
start with the space
regions and make

00:50:10.181 --> 00:50:12.235
that available as a database.

00:50:12.236 --> 00:50:13.255
This link should be able

00:50:13.256 --> 00:50:14.855
to automatically create analyzing

00:50:14.856 --> 00:50:18.930
the shape files and so on.

00:50:18.931 --> 00:50:21.310
Finally, the security,
privacy, and manageability.

00:50:21.311 --> 00:50:27.245
Regarding privacy, I will
just briefly mention that,

00:50:27.246 --> 00:50:30.570
there is work which is
going on in India Stack

00:50:30.571 --> 00:50:33.960
as part of privacy,
supporting privacy.

00:50:33.961 --> 00:50:35.910
They have something
called a Consent

00:50:35.911 --> 00:50:38.435
layer which they're proposing.

00:50:38.436 --> 00:50:41.430
So, one can look at that and
see how we can adopt it.

00:50:41.431 --> 00:50:42.940
But the idea is that,

00:50:42.941 --> 00:50:44.950
there is a clear
ownership of data,

00:50:44.951 --> 00:50:47.155
who owns data, and
the data can only

00:50:47.156 --> 00:50:50.210
be used with the permission
of the owner of the data.

00:50:50.211 --> 00:50:51.875
So that is basically what we want

00:50:51.876 --> 00:50:54.810
to be able to support out there.

00:50:54.970 --> 00:50:58.440
Also, it will be good
to be able to track.

00:50:58.441 --> 00:51:00.975
If I share data with you,

00:51:00.976 --> 00:51:02.660
you don't share it with someone

00:51:02.661 --> 00:51:04.440
else without my knowledge.

00:51:04.441 --> 00:51:07.215
So you need to also have
a mechanism for doing that.

00:51:07.216 --> 00:51:09.030
So I think those all
can be done now with

00:51:09.031 --> 00:51:15.700
this modern encryption and
Public key, PK technologies.

00:51:15.701 --> 00:51:19.935
So, what we have done is

00:51:19.936 --> 00:51:25.290
these are all concepts at
various levels of maturity.

00:51:25.291 --> 00:51:27.525
Some of it is actually
work in progress,

00:51:27.526 --> 00:51:30.660
and we have actually
been working on

00:51:30.661 --> 00:51:33.940
implementing some of
these. Yes, go ahead.

00:51:33.941 --> 00:51:50.855
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:51:50.856 --> 00:51:57.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So,
basically every device

00:51:57.871 --> 00:52:00.250
in the system needs to,

00:52:00.251 --> 00:52:01.710
"in some sense," have similar

00:52:01.711 --> 00:52:05.110
to our biomedical identification.

00:52:05.111 --> 00:52:08.610
Every device needs to have
an identification which

00:52:08.611 --> 00:52:13.410
is somehow tied to some root
of trust of some kind.

00:52:14.120 --> 00:52:16.525
I think some of these things

00:52:16.526 --> 00:52:18.355
the enterprise level people

00:52:18.356 --> 00:52:20.075
have already solutions for that.

00:52:20.076 --> 00:52:23.980
The question is are they-
like you have these TPMs,

00:52:23.981 --> 00:52:27.370
you have this Trust
Zones et cetera.

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:46.300
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] from the device.
The mechanism [inaudible] ?

00:52:46.301 --> 00:52:49.370
&gt;&gt; The access to the data is,

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:52.570
you have to get
access to the data.

00:52:52.571 --> 00:52:55.340
So, there's the owner
of the data.

00:52:55.341 --> 00:52:56.670
In the case, it
could be the city.

00:52:56.671 --> 00:52:58.200
So, if you want to
access those streams,

00:52:58.201 --> 00:53:00.170
you need to have a relationship

00:53:00.171 --> 00:53:02.235
with the city to get
access to the data.

00:53:02.236 --> 00:53:03.860
Now, if you do some bad things in

00:53:03.861 --> 00:53:05.465
the data, that could happen,

00:53:05.466 --> 00:53:07.605
but there is some very clear,

00:53:07.606 --> 00:53:09.330
shall we say, audit trail.

00:53:09.331 --> 00:53:12.600
As you have taken the data,
it's recorded somewhere.

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:14.670
&gt;&gt; I provide some [inaudible]

00:53:14.671 --> 00:53:20.330
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:25.300
Yeah. I guess, yeah,
it's possible.

00:53:25.301 --> 00:53:31.610
I don't know. I mean,
security is a big topic.

00:53:31.830 --> 00:53:35.290
There could be ways of
hacking around the system.

00:53:35.291 --> 00:53:36.980
But the thing is that at least

00:53:36.981 --> 00:53:39.770
the point is there is
an end-to-end encryption

00:53:39.771 --> 00:53:42.370
with also strong identification

00:53:42.371 --> 00:53:44.630
of devices and humans, right?

00:53:44.631 --> 00:53:46.935
So, there's identity
management system in place,

00:53:46.936 --> 00:53:48.360
and the data is only

00:53:48.361 --> 00:53:50.665
given to authorized
people essentially.

00:53:50.666 --> 00:53:52.610
The owner of the data
authorizes the use of the data.

00:53:52.611 --> 00:53:54.510
So, that is the kind of
framework we can put in.

00:53:54.511 --> 00:53:57.535
&gt;&gt; Sometimes [inaudible]
security and privacy.

00:53:57.536 --> 00:54:01.930
Do you see that
those [inaudible].

00:54:01.931 --> 00:54:03.340
&gt;&gt; See, right now,

00:54:03.341 --> 00:54:05.940
it is at a very high level
if you remember,

00:54:05.941 --> 00:54:09.560
they will say using slot files
and online certificates,

00:54:09.561 --> 00:54:10.995
that is the level.

00:54:10.996 --> 00:54:13.670
So, that is where
the next level of

00:54:13.671 --> 00:54:17.900
detail as to what it should
be in more precise terms.

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.525
&gt;&gt; This is the precise, I mean,

00:54:20.526 --> 00:54:22.130
this is an implementation
detail, right?

00:54:22.131 --> 00:54:23.360
What do I care about whether it's

00:54:23.361 --> 00:54:25.490
using one certificate,
another certificate.

00:54:25.491 --> 00:54:27.770
I want to know what my
end-to-end guarantees,

00:54:27.771 --> 00:54:29.445
and then work backwards and

00:54:29.446 --> 00:54:31.690
figure out what cryptographic
protocols are needed.

00:54:31.691 --> 00:54:33.110
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:37.130
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] has
started themes.

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:45.930
&gt;&gt; You're right. The certificates

00:54:45.931 --> 00:54:49.070
are one way of implementing
security. I agree with you.

00:54:49.071 --> 00:54:53.125
So, what we are
actually doing is that,

00:54:53.126 --> 00:54:56.245
at a high level, you want
encrypted communication.

00:54:56.246 --> 00:54:57.805
That is a high level requirement.

00:54:57.806 --> 00:54:59.230
But how do you achieve

00:54:59.231 --> 00:55:00.980
that encrypted
communication, right?

00:55:00.981 --> 00:55:05.800
So, what we want to actually
propose now, essentially,

00:55:05.801 --> 00:55:08.130
what we are doing is,
I'll just come to that,

00:55:08.131 --> 00:55:09.190
but there the consortium we are

00:55:09.191 --> 00:55:10.405
making where we're coming up

00:55:10.406 --> 00:55:13.040
with these more
detailed specification.

00:55:13.041 --> 00:55:17.380
Some of it on the security side
is on the implementation,

00:55:17.381 --> 00:55:19.170
probably, it is at the flavor
of implementation.

00:55:19.171 --> 00:55:24.915
Use this, yeah, so
that's what it is, yes.

00:55:24.916 --> 00:55:28.090
So, there's a lot of POC work,

